"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 16.5 and an ISUP grade group 3 Gleason 4+3 identified both at biopsy and prostatectomy. Contributing to risk are positive surgical margins and the pathological T3a stage. Countervailing findings include no documented extraprostatic extension, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, negative lymphovascular invasion, and no detected lymph node metastasis. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows consistency in Gleason grading (4+3). Missing information regarding postoperative PSA trajectory significantly widens the uncertainty surrounding recurrence risk. Further data on subsequent PSA levels would be crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 51.41 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 38.56–64.26 months; its coverage has not been validated."
